140: \(y\ne0\), the coefficient arrow from the Cartier frames to the
141: standard dual-tautological and tautological frames is exactly
142: \[
143:                          \operatorname{diag}(1/y,y).      \tag{SP.8}
144: \]
145: Indeed the first pulled-back divisor frame is \(y/x\), and the
146: second divisor is absent from this chart. Equation (SP.8) follows by
147: expressing \(1/x\) and \(y\) in those frames. The corresponding
148: equation on \(x\ne0\) agrees on the overlap.
149: 
150: The actual Cox quaternion is
151: \((1+jZ)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\). Its summand order is
152: \((c_0,\overline{c_1})=(\text{second},\text{first})\) in
153: (SP.7). After the positive Hermitian normalization of (SP.8), a
154: Cartier vector \((a,b)\) therefore becomes
155: \((\operatorname{phase}(y)b,\overline{\operatorname{phase}(y)}a)\)
156: in Cox order. Multiplication by the normalized first quaternion gives
157: \[
158:  g(x,y)=\frac1{\sqrt{|x|^2+|y|^2}}
159:        \begin{pmatrix}y&-\overline x\\x&\overline y\end{pmatrix}
160:                                                                \tag{SP.9}
161: \]
162: times the fixed summand swap. In particular (SP.9) is the
163: \textbf{Cartier-coordinate to Cox-coordinate} arrow. It is not its
164: inverse. A constant unitary adjustment of the swap makes it
165: determinant one and does not change its homotopy class.
166: 
167: The same fixed swap must also be kept on real normal coordinates.
168: Its adjoint action is
169: \[
170:                   (\gamma,\ell)\longmapsto
171:                            (-\gamma,\overline\ell).       \tag{SP.10}
172: \]
173: Both the real line and the complex plane reverse orientation;
174: their rank-three product preserves orientation. This is precisely
175: F56's constant positive-Hopf normal identification, retained at both
176: caps.
177: 
178: The inner projection-normal frame is the Cartier frame used here,
179: up to an extending gauge. To verify that assertion rather than
180: postulate it, use F56's explicit nonzero normal covector
181: \[
